/**
 * @module ast
 *
 * The {Node} type `zuri.parse()` returns: a generic
 * representation of one AST node, tagged by `kind` (`'Binary'`,
 * `'If'`, `'Function'`, ...) with a `fields` dict holding
 * whatever that kind carries. There is one `Node` class rather
 * than one class per grammar rule, since the grammar has roughly
 * fifty expression/statement/declaration shapes, and a dedicated
 * class per shape would mean fifty near- identical classes to
 * maintain in lockstep with the parser forever after. A `fields`
 * entry that's itself node-shaped (or a list of node-shaped
 * entries) is already wrapped into a {Node} recursively, so you
 * never need to call `Node.from_dict()` yourself.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * for node in zuri.parse('def add(a, b) { return a + b }') {
 *   echo node.kind
 * }
 * # Function
 * ```
 *
 * ## Comments and doc blocks
 *
 * `parse()`'s whole point, beyond exposing the grammar itself,
 * is that its result is complete enough to rebuild the source
 * file from. Every `#`-comment and doc block comment in the file
 * comes back as its own `'Comment'`/`'DocBlock'`-kind node,
 * sitting exactly where it appeared: as a sibling in the
 * surrounding statement/declaration list, at the top level,
 * inside a `{ }` block, or between two class members.
 *
 * A comment written inside an expression is NOT lost, but it
 * isn't attached inside that expression's own node either.
 * There's no `Node` kind for "a comment sitting between two call
 * arguments", and there never will be, since CLAUDE.md's own
 * Zuri style guide never places a comment there in the first
 * place (a comment is always its own whole line, between
 * statements/declarations/class members, not mid-expression).
 * What actually happens: the comment is captured as a standalone
 * `'Comment'`/`'DocBlock'` node and placed as a sibling of the
 * SMALLEST enclosing statement/declaration/class-member, in that
 * same surrounding list, immediately after it (before whatever
 * real node follows in that list, or at the list's own end if
 * nothing does). It stays exactly as deeply nested as the
 * statement that contained it; a comment inside an `if` body
 * three blocks deep comes back three blocks deep too, never
 * hoisted to the top.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var source = "def foo() {\n  echo bar(a, # x\n  b)\n  echo 2\n}\n"
 * var body = zuri.parse(source)[0].fields.body.fields.statements
 * echo body[0].kind        # Echo, the statement the comment sat inside
 * echo body[1].kind        # Comment, landed right after it, not inside it
 * echo body[1].fields.text # ' x'
 * echo body[2].kind        # Echo, the "echo 2" line, unaffected
 * ```
 *
 * Source written against CLAUDE.md's own comment-placement
 * convention is completely unaffected by this: every comment in
 * real, style-conformant Zuri source is already its own whole
 * line between statements, which is exactly the case `parse()`
 * places correctly, in its original position. This only matters
 * for source that breaks that convention, and even then, nothing
 * is thrown away; the comment's `line`/`col` still point at
 * where it actually appeared, it's just not a descendant of the
 * expression node it visually sat inside.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var source = "# a helper function\ndef add(a, b) {\n  return a + b\n}\n"
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 * echo nodes[0].kind        # Comment
 * echo nodes[0].fields.text # " a helper function"
 * echo nodes[1].kind        # Function
 * ```
 *
 * A class body's fields and methods are two separate lists
 * (`Decl::Class`'s own shape, mirrored here as a `Class` node's
 * `properties`/`methods` fields), so a comment between a field
 * and a method lands in `methods`, whichever list the member
 * right after it belongs to, and a comment between two fields
 * lands in `properties`. See `Node`'s own field-by-field notes
 * below. Code that wants the true interleaved order across both
 * lists, to rebuild a class body exactly as written, should
 * merge `properties ++ methods` and sort by `(line, col)`.
 *
 * @copyright 2026, Richard Ore and Zuri contributors
 */

import _zuri_parse
import ._tree

/**
 * One AST node: an expression, statement, declaration, type
 * hint, or a standalone comment/doc block sitting between two of
 * those.
 *
 * @printable
 */
class Node {

  /**
   * Returns a new instance of a {Node} directly, from
   * already-wrapped field values. Ordinary Zuri code should use
   * `Node.from_dict()` or `zuri.parse()` instead, since this
   * constructor does no wrapping of its own.
   *
   * @param string kind: The node's kind, e.g. `'Binary'`, `'If'`
   * @param ?number line: The node's source line, or `nil` if
   *    this kind of node carries no position (see `from_dict`'s
   *    own docs)
   * @param ?number col: The node's source column, or `nil`
   * @param dict fields: This kind's own fields, already wrapped
   */
  @new(kind, line, col, fields) {
    self.kind = kind
    self.line = line
    self.col = col
    self.fields = fields
  }

  /**
   * Builds a {Node} from the raw dict `zuri.parse()`'s native
   * backing produces (`{ kind, line, col, fields }`),
   * recursively wrapping any node-shaped entry inside `fields`,
   * a single nested node or a list of them, into its own {Node}.
   * A `fields` entry that isn't node-shaped (a string, a number,
   * a bool, `nil`, or a plain dict like a `Var` node's
   * `type_hint` when there isn't one) passes through unchanged.
   *
   * `line`/`col` are best-effort, not guaranteed on every node.
   * Most AST shapes carry a full position (an identifier, an
   * operator, a declaration's own name); a few (`Binary`,
   * `Call`, `Index`, and other operator/delimiter-driven
   * expressions) only ever carry a bare source `line`, with
   * `col` always `nil`; and a handful of purely structural nodes
   * (an empty `Block`, `None`) carry neither, coming back with
   * `line`/`col` both `nil`. None of this affects what `fields`
   * contains, since position is a convenience for pointing at
   * where something is, not part of the information rebuilding
   * source needs.
   *
   * @param dict data: `{ kind, line, col, fields }`
   * @returns Node
   */
  static from_dict(data) {
    var fields = {}
    for key in data.fields.keys() {
      fields[key] = _wrap_node(data.fields[key])
    }
    return Node(data.kind, data.line, data.col, fields)
  }

  /**
   * Is this node a standalone comment or doc block (as opposed
   * to a real expression/statement/declaration)? See this
   * module's own doc comment for exactly where these can and
   * can't appear.
   *
   * @returns bool
   */
  is_trivia() {
    return self.kind == 'Comment' or self.kind == 'DocBlock'
  }

  /**
   * This node as `kind@line:col`, e.g. `'Binary@3:5'`;
   * `kind@line` when a position exists but has no column (some
   * AST shapes only ever carry a bare source line, never a
   * column: see `from_dict`'s own docs); or just `kind` when it
   * carries no position at all. A one-line summary; use `dump()`
   * to actually see what's inside a node.
   * @returns string
   */
  to_string() {
    return _header(self)
  }

  @to_string() {
    return self.to_string()
  }

  /**
   * A readable, indented, multi-line dump of this node and
   * everything inside it, recursively. This is the actual way to
   * find out what a `fields` entry is called for a kind you
   * haven't looked up yet: `echo node.dump()` and read it,
   * rather than guessing a key name and checking whether it
   * happens to exist.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * import zuri
   *
   * var nodes = zuri.parse('def add(a, b) { return a + b }')
   * echo nodes[0].dump()
   * ```
   * prints
   * ```
   * Function@1:5
   *   name: 'add'
   *   parameters:
   *     Argument@1:9
   *       name: 'a'
   *       type_hint:
   *         TypeHint
   *           types:
   *             Any
   *               class_name: nil
   *           nullable: true
   *     Argument@1:12
   *       name: 'b'
   *       type_hint:
   *         TypeHint
   *           types:
   *             Any
   *               class_name: nil
   *           nullable: true
   *   body:
   *     Block@1
   *       statements:
   *         Return@1
   *           value:
   *             Binary@1
   *               left:
   *                 Identifier@1:24
   *                   name: 'a'
   *               op: 'Plus'
   *               right:
   *                 Identifier@1:28
   *                   name: 'b'
   *   is_variadic: false
   * ```
   *
   * `Block`/`Return`/`Binary` above only carry a line, never a
   * column (see `from_dict`'s own docs on which shapes do and
   * don't); `class_name` is `nil` on `Any` specifically because
   * that field only ever has a real value on an `Instance` type
   * (a named class used as a type hint).
   *
   * @returns string
   */
  dump() {
    return "\n".join(_dump_lines(self, 0))
  }
}

/**
 * Recursively wraps a raw value from a node's `fields` dict: a
 * node-shaped dict becomes a {Node}, a list has this applied to
 * each of its own entries, and everything else (a string,
 * number, bool, `nil`, or a plain non-node dict) passes through
 * as-is.
 *
 * A dict counts as "node-shaped" when it carries both a `kind`
 * and a `fields` entry, the two keys nothing else `zuri.parse()`
 * produces happens to have together, so this can't misfire on a
 * `TypeHint`'s plain `{ types, nullable }` fields dict or
 * similar.
 */
def _wrap_node(value) {
  if is_list(value) {
    var result = []
    for item in value {
      result.append(_wrap_node(item))
    }
    return result
  }

  if is_dict(value) and value.get('kind', nil) != nil and value.get('fields', nil) != nil {
    return Node.from_dict(value)
  }

  return value
}

# --- dump() support ---
#
# Builds a list of already-indented lines rather than one long string,
# so a nested call can just `.extend()` the caller's own list instead
# of juggling partial-line concatenation.

def _indent(level) {
  var s = ''
  iter var i = 0; i < level; i++ {
    s = s + '  '
  }
  return s
}

def _dump_scalar(value) {
  if value == nil {
    return 'nil'
  }
  if is_string(value) {
    return "'" + value + "'"
  }
  return '${value}'
}

# Shared by `Node.to_string()` and `dump()`'s own node header line;
# `line` without `col` is a real, common case (see `from_dict`'s
# docs), not just a hypothetical to guard against.
def _header(node) {
  if node.line == nil {
    return node.kind
  }
  if node.col == nil {
    return '${node.kind}@${node.line}'
  }
  return '${node.kind}@${node.line}:${node.col}'
}

def _dump_lines(value, indent) {
  if instance_of(value, Node) {
    var lines = [_indent(indent) + _header(value)]
    for key in value.fields.keys() {
      var field_value = value.fields[key]
      if instance_of(field_value, Node) or is_list(field_value) {
        lines.append(_indent(indent + 1) + key + ':')
        lines.extend(_dump_lines(field_value, indent + 2))
      } else {
        lines.append(_indent(indent + 1) + key + ': ' + _dump_scalar(field_value))
      }
    }
    return lines
  }

  if is_list(value) {
    if value.is_empty() {
      return [_indent(indent) + '[]']
    }
    var lines = []
    for item in value {
      lines.extend(_dump_lines(item, indent))
    }
    return lines
  }

  return [_indent(indent) + _dump_scalar(value)]
}

/**
 * Parses `source` in full and returns its top-level declarations
 * as a list of {Node}. See this module's own doc comment for the
 * exact shape, and in particular for what is and isn't preserved
 * around comments.
 *
 * Raises (rather than returning a partial result) on a syntax
 * error, since there is no meaningful AST to hand back for
 * source the parser itself couldn't make sense of.
 *
 * @param string source: The Zuri source to parse
 * @returns list[Node]
 * @throws Error if `source` has a syntax error
 */
def parse(source) {
  var nodes = []
  for raw in _zuri_parse.parse(source) {
    nodes.append(Node.from_dict(raw))
  }
  return nodes
}

/**
 * Reads the file at `path` and parses its contents; exactly
 * `parse(file(path).read())`, for the common case of parsing a
 * real file rather than a source string you've already got in
 * hand.
 *
 * @param string path: Path to the Zuri source file to parse
 * @returns list[Node]
 * @throws Error if `path` can't be opened or read, or has a
 *    syntax error
 */
def parse_file(path) {
  return parse(file(path).read())
}

/**
 * Reads, parses, and dumps the file at `path` in one call: the
 * fastest way to answer "what does this file's AST actually look
 * like". Equivalent to parsing `path` and joining every
 * resulting top-level node's own `dump()` with a blank line
 * between them.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 * echo zuri.dump_file('main.zu')
 * ```
 *
 * @param string path: Path to the Zuri source file to parse and
 *    dump
 * @returns string
 * @throws Error if `path` can't be opened or read, or has a
 *    syntax error
 */
def dump_file(path) {
  var dumps = []
  for node in parse_file(path) {
    dumps.append(node.dump())
  }
  return "\n\n".join(dumps)
}

def _describe(value) {
  if instance_of(value, Node) {
    return value.kind
  }
  return nil
}

/**
 * Recursively visits every node reachable from `nodes`
 * (typically `zuri.parse()`'s own result, or any single {Node}
 * plucked out of it), in the order they appear, calling
 * `visitor` for each one. This is the tool for "do something
 * with every `X`": most real uses of an AST are a walk, not
 * manual field-by-field navigation.
 *
 * `visitor` is either a plain function, called with every node
 * (`visitor(node)`), or a dict from kind name to function, where
 * only a node of that kind gets called
 * (`visitor[node.kind](node)`). Reach for the dict form when you
 * only care about one or two kinds; it reads as a small dispatch
 * table instead of a chain of `if node.kind == ...` checks
 * inside a single function.
 *
 * Always visits the whole tree; there's no way for `visitor` to
 * skip descending into a particular node's children.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 *
 * # every function name declared anywhere in the file, including
 * # nested/inner functions
 * var names = []
 * zuri.walk_nodes(nodes, {
 *   Function: @(node) { names.append(node.fields.name) },
 * })
 * ```
 *
 * @param Node|list[Node] nodes
 * @param function|dict visitor
 */
def walk_nodes(nodes, visitor) {
  _tree.walk(nodes, _describe, visitor, is_dict(visitor))
}

/**
 * Every node reachable from `nodes` whose `kind` is `kind`, in
 * the order they appear. Shorthand for the common "just give me
 * a flat list of these" case `walk_nodes()` itself is built on.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 * for comment in zuri.find_nodes(nodes, 'Comment') {
 *   echo comment.fields.text
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * @param Node|list[Node] nodes
 * @param string kind
 * @returns list[Node]
 */
def find_nodes(nodes, kind) {
  return _tree.find(nodes, _describe, kind)
  return matches
}
